{{smaller block|⟦300⟧“For the benefit of those who may glance at the appendix before they read the paper, I will mention that <math>\text{T}</math> is the number of seconds occupied by the ship in performing a single oscillation in still water, starboard to port, or vice versa. <math>\text{T}_1</math> is the number of seconds occupied by the wave in passing from hollow to crest, or crest to hollow. <math>\Theta_1</math> is the number of degrees in slope of the steepest part of the wave; and <math>p/q</math> is the ratio <math>\text{T}/\text{T}_1</math>, with the numerator and denominator converted into the lowest whole numbers that will express the ratio, where, however, it must be noticed that for <math>\text{T}/\text{T}_1 = 1</math>, <math>p/q</math> must be taken as the limit of such a form as <math>\tfrac{999999}{1000000}</math>. Then—}}

{{smaller block|⟦600⟧“(i.) The ship will complete the phase in the time <math>= 2q\text{T}</math>.}}

{{smaller block|⟦600⟧“(ii.) In completing the phase the ship will pass through the vertical position <math>2\ p</math> times, or <math>2\ q</math> times, according as <math>p</math> or <math>q</math> is the smaller number.}}

{{smaller block|⟦700⟧“(iii.) The ship will pass through the vertical position at the middle of the phase.}}

{{smaller block|⟦800⟧“(iv.) On either side of the middle of the phase there must occur, as equal maximum oscillation, the maximum in the phase, say <math>\Theta</math>, which will approximately (but never in excess) <math>= \pm\Theta_1\frac{q}{q-p}</math>.}}

{{smaller block|⟦900⟧“(v.) From these propositions it appears that if we compare two cases, in one of which the value of <math>\text{T}/\text{T}_1</math> is the reciprocal of its value in the other, the phase will in each case consist of the same number of oscillations similarly placed; but in that one in which the period of the wave is slower than the period of the ship, the angles of oscillation will be the larger in the ratio <math>p/q</math> or <math>q/p</math>, whichever is the greater. The following table expresses the results of the above propositions, as exhibited in the diagrams, based on the assumption that the period of the ship is in every case <math>\text{T} = 5''</math>, and that the maximum slope of the wave <math>\Theta_1 = 9</math> degrees:}}
